@@ -0,0 +1,24 @@
+---
+id: dl-001-resolvepointer-throws-on-unresolvable-pointers-and-ignores-inherited-properties
+type: decision-log
+title: "resolvePointer throws on unresolvable pointers and ignores inherited properties"
+status: pending
+---
+
+## Context
+
+`resolvePointer(document, pointer)` implements RFC 6901 and later features build on it. The RFC leaves
+some behaviours to the implementation: what an unresolvable pointer yields, and the `-` token.
+
+## Decision
+
+- Any invalid pointer, or pointer that does not resolve, throws an `Error`; there is no `undefined` return.
+- Only own members are resolved: `/__proto__`, `/constructor`, `/length` on arrays etc. throw.
+- On arrays, a token must match `0|[1-9][0-9]*` and be in range; `-` (the element past the end) does not
+  resolve and throws. On objects `-` and numeric-looking tokens are ordinary keys.
+- `~` must be followed by `0` or `1`; unescaping is `~1` then `~0`, so `~01` is `~1`.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Callers can distinguish a found `null` from a missing value. Later features that need `-` (e.g. patch
+add) must handle it themselves. Inherited-property lookups cannot leak prototype data.
